### 2.2. Scene-Grounded Evaluation with 3DGS

A scene-grounded evaluation substrate should be reconstructed from real captures while still allowing controlled rendering under new viewpoints, distances, and camera conditions. Classical structure-from-motion and multiview stereo estimate camera poses and scene structure from image collections (Schonberger and Frahm, [2016](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib59 "Structure-from-motion revisited"); Schönberger et al., [2016](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib60 "Pixelwise view selection for unstructured multi-view stereo")). Neural rendering methods such as NeRF and its efficient variants turn multiview captures into novel-view renderers (Mildenhall et al., [2021](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib55 "Nerf: representing scenes as neural radiance fields for view synthesis"); Müller et al., [2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib56 "Instant neural graphics primitives with a multiresolution hash encoding"); Barron et al., [2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib57 "Mip-nerf 360: unbounded anti-aliased neural radiance fields"); Haque et al., [2023](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib9 "Instruct-nerf2nerf: editing 3d scenes with instructions")). We use 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) (Kerbl et al., [2023](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib1 "3D gaussian splatting for real-time radiance field rendering"); Huang et al., [2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib7 "2D gaussian splatting for geometrically accurate radiance fields")) because it provides high-fidelity reconstruction and efficient rendering.

A 3DGS scene represents the scene as a set of anisotropic Gaussian primitives, G={gi}i=1N,G=\{g\_{i}\}\_{i=1}^{N}, where each Gaussian gig\_{i} is parameterized by its center μi\mu\_{i}, covariance or scale-rotation parameters, opacity αi\alpha\_{i}, and appearance coefficients cic\_{i}. Given a camera pose 𝜽\bm{\theta}, a differentiable renderer ℛ\mathcal{R} produces an image I​(𝜽)=ℛ​(G,𝜽).I(\bm{\theta})=\mathcal{R}(G,\bm{\theta}). Because 3DGS can be reconstructed from multiview real captures and rendered from novel viewpoints, it can, in principle, combine the grounding of real scenes with the controllability needed for operational measurement.

