Standard 3DGS editing is not sufficient for adversarial patch evaluation. Existing editing methods primarily target photorealistic or semantic modification, such as object insertion, texture editing, or localized appearance transfer (Chen et al., [2024](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib2 "GaussianEditor: swift and controllable 3d editing with gaussian splatting"); Cao et al., [2026](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib3 "3DOT: texture transfer for 3dgs objects from a single reference image"); Xiao et al., [2025](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib4 "Localized gaussian splatting editing with contextual awareness"); Galerne et al., [2025](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#bib.bib5 "SGSST: scaling gaussian splatting style transfer")). In contrast, adversarial evaluation requires preserving attack-critical image details while keeping the modification localized and surface-attached. Small changes in color, opacity, boundary alignment, or cross-view placement can alter model behavior even when the edit appears visually plausible to humans.

## 3. AdvScene: Operational Measurement Framework

Given the above threat model, AdvScene turns a fixed anchor-view attack into a scene-grounded measurement pipeline. Its input is a clean multiview capture, an attacked anchor image Ia⋆I\_{a}^{\star}, and a patch mask MaM\_{a}; its output is an adversarial scene GadvG\_{\mathrm{adv}} and rendered observations used to estimate the operational envelope.

