### 3.1. End-to-End Pipeline

AdvScene realizes these requirements through the four-stage pipeline shown in Figure [2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2605.30578#S2.F2 "Figure 2 ‣ 2.1. Physical Patch Attacks and Evaluation Challenge ‣ 2. Background and Threat Model ‣ AdvScene: Rethinking Adversarial Patch Evaluation Through Scene Robustness"). Stage 1: Scene reconstruction reconstructs a clean 3DGS scene G0G\_{0} from multiview images of a clean real scene, providing the scene-grounded foundation for controllable rendering. Stage 2: Anchor attack instantiation selects an anchor view aa and applies an external adversarial patch generation pipeline to obtain the attacked image Ia⋆I\_{a}^{\star} and patch mask MaM\_{a}. Stage 3: Adversarial Patch-to-Scene Embedding (APSE) lifts the attacked anchor-view appearance into the reconstructed scene, producing an adversarial scene GadvG\_{\mathrm{adv}} that preserves attack-critical appearance while keeping the patch localized on the target surface. Stage 4: Scene-level robustness evaluation renders GadvG\_{\mathrm{adv}} under controlled observation conditions and evaluates the victim model on the resulting images. This produces a scene-grounded measurement of where an adversarial patch succeeds and where it fails.

